1071 "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $y = (\\frac{3}{5})^{x}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111140 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "efa980a6-6790-54ec-92bb-d5c383a444ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111604 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111604 #edb/ref 1061 3935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111604 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fa05b5ee-1d45-5481-a6c9-e019dfaa173b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111604 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111605 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111605 #edb/ref 1061 3936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111605 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "41927360-32e5-5db6-a9f3-26f71dd06db5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111605 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111606 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111606 #edb/ref 1061 3939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111606 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "04a9b12e-b969-57f1-a5e7-19bc2fc561a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111606 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111607 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111607 #edb/ref 1061 3940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111607 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "adbf259f-fa2f-5745-bd7e-7048bd565045" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111607 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111608 #edb/ref 1061 3943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111608 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9dfe9d61-57bc-5cba-b15e-a9df1319f56e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111608 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111709 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111709 #edb/ref 1061 6235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111709 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2cc2ead9-f661-50ca-96a5-5747e77f1192" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111709 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111827 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $y=0.5^x$. First, we create a table of values:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y = 0.5^x$ | $8$ | $4$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ | $0.125$ |\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/b1/b17b1e0f554b8da98f46b89265cbec3be2fab1a40b6c04ef03f5b4cec94ac3d8.png)\n\nNote the following key points:\n\n1. The graph has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1)$.\n2. The graph is decreasing.\n3. For $x<0$, the graph grows very rapidly as $x$ decreases. Symbolically, we can write $y\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty$.\n4. For $x>0$, the graph gets very close to zero as $x$ increases. However, it never actually reaches zero, so the graph has a horizontal asymptote $y=0$. Symbolically, we can write $y\\rightarrow 0$ as $x\\rightarrow \\infty$.\n5. The domain is $x\\in\\left(-\\infty, \\infty\\right)$, and the range is $y>0$.\n\nThese key points apply to *any* exponential curve $y=a^x$ with $0 < a < 1$. These graphs always have the same general shape:\n\n![](images/6c/6c5e94fdb76c1af54dcd1cd8d44bb532ab5c69a305465282b62c28ef2b54ab75.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111827 #edb/ref 1065 1544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111827 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6e919c9a-e896-5b0b-8570-5a7a377146d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111827 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111828 #edb/ref 1064 "Multiplying an exponential decay function by a positive number **scales** the graph in the vertical direction.\n\nFor example, the curve\n\n$$\ny=2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nrepresents a vertical scaling of\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nby a factor of $2$.\nIt takes all of the $y$-coordinates on\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nand multiplies them by $2$. This has the effect of **stretching** the curve upwards.\n\n![](images/36/3654463dd3914bb65ffe58be20cdf2bc4cd19e5a51ac479f4aaca50e2709a214.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remained the same, and the $y$-intercept moved from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2)$.\n\nOn the other hand, the curve\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\left(2^x\\right)\n$$\n\nrepresents a vertical scaling of\n$y = 2^x$\nby a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nIt takes all of the $y$-coordinates on $y = 2^x$ and multiplies them by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nThis has the effect of **compressing** the curve downwards.\n\nIn general, for *any* vertically stretched or compressed exponential curve $y=k(a^x)$,\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remains the same, and\n- the $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111828 #edb/ref 1065 1545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111828 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4ea28190-d93f-521e-964b-cdd4aad84f69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111828 #edb/ref 1067 "Vertical Stretching and Compression" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282086 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{35}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282086 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282086 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2cc34e3d-7f5a-5410-9872-37c1fdc40860" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282370 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{49}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282370 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282370 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4f619c4c-928e-5b2c-a2f6-9650ca210e41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282669 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{7}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282669 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186282669 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "726c1b73-a586-5d60-b744-3f50a3ac17d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283393 #edb/ref 1080 "7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283393 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283393 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c39485e4-e8d5-5bd1-a275-66e3b3650f70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283608 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{28}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283608 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283608 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dbd21f5f-8b88-5095-9dca-4fe1f18d7abe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284174 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284174 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284174 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186283393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284174 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8c0bec54-298c-5799-a502-9235e4f8885b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284583 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284583 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167338" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284583 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284583 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ec/ec6deda5b2400258af6ad455cdfa0072deb754391feb4d916082568c248d95ee.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}.$ What is the value of $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284583 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f5ee281-743c-52e5-8ba6-b17dfb13fd85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284583 #edb/ref 1074 "To plot the graph of $y=k{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,7)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297222 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451137 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186297222 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 1759